inline.s:
At Twin Groves I knocked at evening's door,
and old friends clasped sleeves, their hearts at one.
A year apart, we missed the Longhua Assembly;
far off, our letters stopped when the wild geese failed.
Thatched on Mt. Lao, he had gone back to the north sea;
flowers opened on Yu Ridge, toward the southern bough.
Hengyang's land is narrow, and a monk's robe wide;
clouds lie deep on Mt. Kuang, and the staff has moved.
The terrace mirror is empty by nature; he beheld his own self.
Wind and banner suddenly stirred, and men thought of Huineng.
How often the cool moon doubled a clear dream,
one night of autumn wind, face to face with an old friend.
Leaning at his armrest he talked of heaven, gathering hidden meanings;
wielding the brush, he showed his ground and swept away a host of doubts.
Long free of sticky bonds, his mind ever in samādhi;
ympty of all but compassion, his head bowed again and again.
Deep in autumn's fallen leaves, he forgot the weariness of speech;
hearing the bell at midnight, he spoke of the peril of the heart.
Ever he sighed that the dusty world's heart is always bitter,
and that on the Chan terrace the road turns aside.
Hearing the Dharma words, one could only answer, Yes, yes;
the traveler's road urged him on, and so he was ever slow.
The old know that lakes and seas are hard to meet again;
we pledged the karmic hour, then suddenly drifted apart.
The wild goose passes Cold Mountain; autumn's shadow ends.
The horse neighs at Caoxi; the sound of its going is sad.
A foot of white silk carries a letter; a man faces north.
The ācārya hears the news of death, and tears cross his cheeks.
Hard it is to find timber to prop the Dharma gate;
the boat hidden in the sea of enlightenment cannot be chased.
The sleeping butterfly, startled, has only now entered dream;
the dragon soars and coils — where would he go?
To meet him now is already to have passed a thousand kalpas;
to be transformed away needs no word of praise.
Forgetting self, one does not covet the marks of the formless;
beholding emptiness, marvel not at the wonder of form.
Looking back at the peak's colors, the temple at the wood's edge;
I dream of the sound of the tide, poems in the box.
Quiescence never divided going from staying;
his solemn dignity does not alter its old bearing.
My one grief is that Dharma companions must grow few —
to whom now shall the bowl be entrusted, the robe passed on?

Returning to the end, dragons and devas have the Great Master;
from the lecture seat heavenly flowers fell, drifting late.
From the kitchen came the Dharma meal, from the Compassion Palace;
beyond the ridges his grace flowed, and the sage ruler knew it.
Gulls and egrets, and officials, wondered that he played with the world;
sandalwood and the sea treasury had their season of scripture.
Who is his chief disciple, one like Kāśyapa,
to take up and compose the testament I grieve for?

Drop by drop Caoxi weeps for Nanhua;
in those days I myself received the sitting cloth of gauze.
The mind seal was transmitted alone, without a single word;
the golden ground was spread anew with three vehicles.
Forest wind and moon veil the lamp by the bed;
valley rain and mist dissolve the tea after samādhi.
The Dharma's revival in the final age has fallen once more;
lingering by the twin trees, I sigh alone and long.

The prophecy of the Southern School is a thousand years old;
at this way-place he settled once more into Chan.
The Dharma stream lives in the heart, in endless awakening;
the Patriarch has gone, the robe is hidden, never to be transmitted again.
Weeping breaks off the bhikṣu beneath the yellow leaves;
the spirit is buried by the locked gate, beside the white clouds.
He must have left a Bequeathed Teaching Sūtra, equal to the Buddha's;
still, from midstream, one gains the jewel boat.

In that year of the full moon I first tried the Chan of inquiry;
taking refuge at the first ground, I remember Hunan.
The robe was of white felt, and the body ever pure;
the teaching set forth the blue lotus, the tongue containing it again.
Golden plates translated to the end of how many scriptures;
silver hooks wrote out a thousand cases of the Patriarch — (when I had the Master's Surangama Commentary engraved, the Master in turn wrote out for me with his own hand a portrait eulogy of the Patriarch and a biography.)
Who is the true benefactor who has failed the Master?
Three times I sent letters of invitation, and I am ashamed of myself alone.

A thread of spiritual light draws us each to each;
before Eight-Li Village, twenty years ago.
Today I bow to the Master, and his bones are still here;
I know the Master was always an ancient golden immortal.

In the blessed ground of Nanhua his whole body is enshrined;
beneath his feet he still wears the clouds of Wuru.
His staff churned the clear world into mud;
who knows how many men obtained the Dharma?

Weeping over a sealed letter, my heart turns restless;
in vain I write to his hand's traces, pleading the old pledge.
If we had met, we would not have grudged a thousand years hung between;
giving oneself up, one must know, betrays a whole life.
The ancients are praised only for carrying their shoes away;
at a deathbed, who understands how to bray like a donkey?
Bleak is the traveler's inn in the last snows of winter;
the specks on my sleeve cling to me as if they had feeling.

Tears fall to the edge of heaven, sent to my brothers;
the late Master's testament is all too clear.
Brows and beard of the Five Elders can be pictured;
waist and spine of three ridges come of themselves.
Red leaves: the blood of both eyes of a man of this land.
White clouds: a disciple's single-minded banner.
Here I receive the earnest meaning;
why need we talk grandly of death and life?
